{
  "id": "number-of-operations-to-connect-network",
  "questionId": "number-of-operations-to-connect-network",
  "slug": "number-of-operations-to-connect-network",
  "title": "Minimum Cables to Fully Connect a Network",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Union find",
  "patterns": [
    "union find",
    "connected components"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "if there are fewer than n - 1 cables total, there's no way to connect everything - check that first",
    "otherwise, union the endpoints of every cable to find how many separate groups of computers exist",
    "connecting k separate groups into one always takes exactly k - 1 moves, since each move can only merge two groups into one",
    "count the number of distinct roots after all unions, and subtract 1"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting the early -1 check when connections.length < n - 1",
    "trying to figure out exactly which cable goes where instead of just counting components",
    "off-by-one when converting component count into number of moves"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A network has n computers, labeled 0 to n-1, and a list of existing `connections`, where each cable is a pair `[u, v]` directly connecting two computers. You can unplug any existing cable and use it to connect any two computers of your choice instead.\n\n**Return the minimum number of such moves needed to make every computer reachable from every other computer, or -1 if there simply aren't enough cables to do it no matter how you rearrange them.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 100000",
      "0 <= connections.length <= min(n * (n - 1) / 2, 100000)",
      "connections are undirected, no self loops or repeated pairs"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "already fully connected (0 moves needed)",
      "not enough cables to possibly connect everything",
      "a single computer with no cables at all",
      "several isolated groups requiring multiple moves"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 4, connections = [[0,1],[0,2],[1,2]]",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "computers 0, 1, 2 are connected but 3 is isolated; one spare cable (the redundant 1-2 link) can be moved to connect it"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 6, connections = [[0,1],[0,2],[0,3],[1,2],[1,3]]",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "computers 0-3 form one group, and 4 and 5 are each isolated, needing two moves"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 6, connections = [[0,1],[0,2],[0,3],[1,2]]",
        "output": "-1",
        "expectedOutput": "-1",
        "explanation": "only 4 cables exist but at least 5 are needed to connect 6 computers"
      }
